{"kind":"artifact","text":"The onboarding-identity core, at exact head 520251692928e5f3e912ba454bad460c3a4567b2 on request/onboarding-identity-core, cut from main 92dd2df1. This is the implementation report for request 2c9bf640 under promise 2999b16a; no separate ready-for-review report follows it.\n\nWHAT THE CONDITIONS REQUIRED, AND WHAT IS HERE. The attestation vocabulary: three fixed host schema families, gitseq/identity-witness@0, gitseq/identity-anchor@0 and gitseq/identity-revoke@0, which application profiles cannot rename or extend. GitHub-login witnessing with the log-anchored witness key: DeclareWitness records the public half in the log, admitted only from the key that initialized the repository — the same authority the host binding answers to, since another application has no roster to consult — and identity.GitHub turns a user access token into an identity outside the fold, its result alone being signed in. The two-axis anchored/unanchored resolution model: Resolve folds a verified log into a Resolution, and Lookup(actor, at) answers either the zero Resolved for an unanchored key, which is a complete answer and not a failure, or an anchor carrying Vouching (self-signed over witnessed) and Verification (in-log over live-lookup) already reduced along any chain of inheritance.\n\nDESIGN. One act, Endorse, carries both rungs, and which rung a record occupies is decided at resolution from the key that signed it, never from anything the payload claims — a record cannot promote itself. Signed by the declared witness it may name an identity; signed by anything else it is a delegation, names none, inherits the endorser's identity, takes the weaker value on each axis, cannot outlive the anchor it rests on, and is withdrawn when that anchor is. A new device and an agent credential are therefore the same act. Resolution is the authority and nothing gates appending: an unauthorized, unparseable, malformed, wrong-repository or identity-claiming-beyond-its-signer record is recorded exactly as signed and resolves to nothing, leaving the previous answer standing, so no appender can make a repository's identities unreadable. Expiry and withdrawal are judged against the sequencer's signed timestamp on the record being folded, never the reader's clock.\n\nBOUNDARY. Built inside ratified decision 8a758fb7: single-operator, trusted-process only. This package authenticates nobody and authorizes nothing; witness custody belongs to the deployment, where every process in the trusted boundary can use it, and that is stated in the package doc and the architecture page rather than implied away. No multi-user auth was built. The two-axis DISPLAY surface is deferred to the chess UI thread (item 5), as the request directs. The Nostr rung is not in this extracted scope; the vocabulary admits it later as a stronger origin without change.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD. gofmt -l clean before commit; go build ./... ; go vet ./... ; go test -count=1 ./... green across 27 packages, the docset gate included.\n\nTESTS. Two suites, both using only what an outside module can use. identity_test.go creates real repositories through the public host surface and walks the onboarding path end to end: a browser-minted key plays unanchored, a GitHub token is checked against a test provider, the witness endorses, and the same key resolves as witnessed/in-log; then an anchored person mints an agent credential and withdraws it. resolve_test.go builds logs by hand so the time rules can be stated exactly, which a same-second repository cannot do. Nineteen behaviours are pinned, including witness authority and scheme bounds, rotation not reaching back, cross-repository replay closed at both records, canonical encoding, and the delegation rules.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE. Green proves nothing about coverage, so nineteen seam mutations were run against the suite. Seventeen were caught immediately. Two survived and both were repaired rather than reported: the provider-ceiling test could not distinguish a bounded read from an unbounded one, because its oversized answer was also malformed, and now sends a well-formed answer past the ceiling that only the ceiling can refuse; and DisallowUnknownFields proved unable to change any outcome, because the canonical re-encode-and-compare already refuses every shape it would have, so it was cut and the comparison left as the single rule, with duplicate-key and wrong-case payloads added to the suite to hold it.\n\nARCHITECTURE. This adds a public package, which is a contract change on the exported-Go-API axis, so docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in this same head: a Host identity subsection under layer 4, a host/identity row in the package-boundary table, and the compatibility axis widened to name both packages. That page is published as a separate candidate artifact at docs. host/identity imports host and no application profile, so the layer-4/5 seam holds.","body":{"branch":"request/onboarding-identity-core","commit":"520251692928e5f3e912ba454bad460c3a4567b2","path":"host"}}